  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Sarcodon imbricatus, commonly known as the shingled hedgehog, is a distinctive fungus characterized by its spiny underside rather than gills or pores. This terrestrial mushroom is a choice edible in some regions and belongs to the Boletopsidaceae family.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Spiny Underside",
      "description": "The most diagnostic feature is the prominent, tooth-like spines (hedgehog-like structures) underneath the cap instead of gills or pores",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap Appearance",
      "description": "Gray to dark brown cap with distinctive overlapping shingle-like scales that give the mushroom its common name",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore Staining",
      "description": "Spines and flesh may stain brown or blackish when bruised, and the spore print is white to grayish",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Sarcodon imbricatus grows on acidic forest floors in coniferous and mixed woodlands throughout New England, preferring areas with rich humus and mossy ground cover.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The spines of this hedgehog fungus can be easily separated from the cap flesh, and in some European countries it is highly prized as an edible mushroom when properly prepared.",
